---
// Figure: restoring a snapshot brings back a deleted value but not an expired one.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import './repl.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="rp-snapshot" title="What a snapshot brings back" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. Every command and reply was observed on Redis 8.10.3 on my laptop, with the price change done by hand and a 25-second wait for the banner to expire.">
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Redis caches the kettle at £39 for an hour and a banner for 20 seconds, then takes a snapshot. The price then changes to £35 in the database and the application deletes the cached kettle. The banner’s 20-second TTL then runs out. Redis is then killed and restarted, and loads the snapshot.</p>
    <p>The banner does not come back, because the snapshot stores each key’s expiry as an absolute time, and that time has passed. The kettle does come back at £39 with almost an hour to live, because the snapshot was taken before the delete. Reads now return the old price until the TTL runs out or the key is deleted again.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { sequenceFigure } from '../shared/sequence.mjs';
  import { snapshotSpec } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="rp-snapshot"]').forEach(root => sequenceFigure(root, snapshotSpec));
</script>
